  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1236 AM CDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nWe will continue to see mid-level ridging dominating the period \nleading to warm and mostly rain free conditions outside of isolated \nseabreeze convection each day. As we approach Thursday night into \nFriday morning, we will see an inverted trough with some associated \nvorticity tracking along the southern periphery of the ridge. This \nwill lead to an influx of moisture in the region, surging PWATs \naround 2.0 inches (75th percentile of climatology). Due to these \nfactors coupled with small scale lift will allow for low rain \nchances (~20%) during the morning into the afternoon on Friday. \n\nWill continue to monitor the need for short-fused heat related \nproducts. We could see peak heat indices of 110 to 115 with dew \npoints in the mid to upper 70s into next week. This translates to \nmoderate to predominately major HeatRisk conditions, though some \nareas of extreme HeatRisk will be possible in the Brush Country. An \nadditional risk for elevated fire weather exists over the Rio Grande \nPlains into the next week due to low RH (<30%) and sustained 20 mph \nwinds gusting up to 30 mph. We strongly encourage the practice of \nheat safety in these conditions. Take frequent breaks and remember \nto stay hydrated, check those backseats, and avoid long exposure in \nthese temperatures. For more information be sure to check out \nweather.gov/heatsafety.",
